# Prune — the decision is about cost, not usage
## What justifies removal
A skill costs its name and description on **every turn of every session**,
whether it fires or not. So the question is not "has anyone used it lately" but
**does the description still earn the space it occupies**.
| Remove when | Do not remove because |
| --- | --- |
| Its description overlaps a neighbour's, so neither fires reliably | It has not fired recently — a rarely-needed skill can still be worth its line |
| The judgement it holds moved into another skill | It is short — length is not the cost, the description is |
| It routes somewhere instead of doing the work | You would write it differently now — that is a rewrite, not a removal |
| The tool it wraps is gone, and nothing replaced it | Nobody remembers why it exists — find out first |
The last "do not" is the one that gets ignored. A skill whose reason nobody
remembers is the exact case where the record has to be read before the delete,
because the reason it was written is not recoverable afterwards.
## Overlap is resolved by merge, not by deletion
When two skills claim the same request, the fix is usually to move the useful
half and then delete — not to delete and assume the neighbour covers it. Check
that the neighbour actually says what the removed one said. The `author` skill
covers where the moved material belongs.
Deleting a skill whose content had no home is how a plugin loses a capability
that nothing announces the loss of.
## Delete every trace, in one commit
A partial removal is worse than none: it leaves references that resolve to
nothing and a catalog that promises what is not there. The full list is in
`references/removal-checklist.md`; the shape is:
1. The skill directory itself, including its tests and frozen baselines.
2. Every neighbour's boundary sentence that names it — those are checked, so a
stale name fails a suite that is not the one you were editing.
3. The inventory and the marketplace catalog entry.
4. The red-suite case set, so nothing measures detection on a tree that is gone.
One commit, because a half-applied removal that is bisected through looks like
two different defects.
## Verify by running the neighbours
After the delete, run the suites of every skill that named the removed one, plus
the path gate. The gate is what catches a reference left in a reference file,
which is the trace most often missed — nothing else reads those paths.
A suite that goes green only because its subject vanished falls under
[the missing-check rule](../verify/SKILL.md#a-missing-check-is-not-a-pass).
## Measure the folder before you argue about it
`scripts/prune.js` reads the installed skill folders and reports what is already
broken there: dangling links, a directory whose name disagrees with its
frontmatter `name`, descriptions past the listing cap, config entries pointing
at nothing. It changes nothing and deletes nothing — the removal decision above
stays a human one, and this only supplies the numbers it is made against.
Its exit code is the part another tool reads, and it has three states because a
clean scan and a scan that never happened are different facts:
| Exit | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| 0 | Scanned, nothing wrong |
| 1 | Scanned, found issues |
| 2 | Could not scan — no config directory to look at |
Folding "could not look" into "found nothing" is the same defect as counting a
vanished check as a passing one, one layer down. A diagnostic that always
returns success cannot be aggregated by anything.
`--json` reports the same counts the table prints, produced by the same
function, so the human view and the machine gate can never disagree about how
many issues there were.
## Boundary
Removing dead code inside a skill is the `cleanup` skill; deciding whether the
skill is any good is the `audit` skill. This skill covers taking one out.
